## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of amortized analysis with a practical growth strategy, highlighting how linear growth increments behave differently from exponential doubling and how memory copies interact with cache locality.

## Key Concepts
- Amortized cost under linear growth
- Block-based capacity increments (capacity increases by B, not a multiplicative factor)
- Cache locality during bulk copies
- Trade-offs between growth frequency and per-copy work

## Code Example
```javascript
class Queue {
  constructor(capacity = B) {
    this.buffer = new Array(capacity);
    this.head = 0;
    this.tail = 0;
    this.size = 0;
  }

  enqueue(item) {
    if (this.size === this.buffer.length) {
      const newBuffer = new Array(this.buffer.length + B);
      for (let i = 0; i < this.size; i++) {
        newBuffer[i] = this.buffer[(this.head + i) % this.buffer.length];
      }
      this.buffer = newBuffer;
      this.head = 0;
      this.tail = this.size;
    }
    this.buffer[this.tail] = item;
    this.tail = (this.tail + 1) % this.buffer.length;
    this.size++;
  }

  dequeue() {
    if (this.size === 0) return undefined;
    const item = this.buffer[this.head];
    this.head = (this.head + 1) % this.buffer.length;
    this.size--;
    return item;
  }
}
```

## B's Influence
- **Total copies**: Larger B reduces growth frequency, cutting total copy work from O(N²) toward O(N) (since copies ≈ N²/(2B))
- **Cache behavior**: Smaller B keeps working set in cache but triggers more frequent expensive copies; larger B improves sequential access patterns during copies but may evict useful cache contents when reallocating

## Why This Is Asked
Maintaining the number of distinct substrings under incremental appends tests knowledge of suffix automata, amortized analysis, and practical update strategies. It highlights how to avoid recomputation and reason about state growth.

## Key Concepts
- Suffix Automaton (SAM) for distinct substrings
- Online extensions and delta = len(cur) - len(link(cur))
- Amortized analysis over batches
- Space linear in string length

## Code Example
```javascript
class SAMNode {
  constructor() {
    this.next = {};
    this.link = -1;
    this.len = 0;
  }
}
class SuffixAutomaton {
  constructor() {
    this.st = [new SAMNode()];
    this.last = 0;
    this.size = 1;
  }
  extend(c){
    const st = this.st; const cur = this.size++;
    st.push(new SAMNode()); st[cur].len = st[this.last].len + 1;
    let p = this.last;
    while (p >= 0 && !(c in st[p].next)) { st[p].next[c] = cur; p = st[p].link; }
    if (p === -1) { st[cur].link = 0; }
    else {
      const q = st[p].next[c];
      if (st[p].len + 1 === st[q].len) {
        st[cur].link = q;
      } else {
        const clone = this.size++;
        st.push(Object.assign(new SAMNode(), st[q]));
        st[clone].len = st[p].len + 1;
        while (p >= 0 && st[p].next[c] === q) { st[p].next[c] = clone; p = st[p].link; }
        st[q].link = st[cur].link = clone;
      }
    }
    this.last = cur;
  }
}
```

